  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

A spatially explicit agent-based model of commute mode choice (car, public transit, bicycle, walking) on the Island of Montreal. It is the NetLogo port of the Mesa notebook `SocialInteractionGraph_MostEfficientVersion.ipynb`, built for the Mesa / repast4py / NetLogo comparison. `PORTING_NOTES.md` (next to this model) documents every porting choice.

Each agent stands for `n-rep` adult commuters of a census Dissemination Area (DA). Agents get an age, a gender, an income drawn from the DA income brackets, a residential parcel in their DA and a workplace within a commute radius drawn from the DA commute-time distribution. Travel times are shortest paths on four OpenStreetMap-derived networks (car, walk, bike, transit; transit = walk to the nearest STM stop + transit + walk from the stop nearest to work). A multinomial-logit utility (33 coefficients) gives each mode a utility from standardised travel time, age, gender, income, local population density, distance to the nearest bike lane, and, for the bicycle, the share of the agent's peers who cycle. Peers are chosen among agents living or working within `interaction-radius` who are most similar in age, gender and income (Gower distance).

## HOW IT WORKS

Each step every agent picks the mode with the highest utility (the argmax of the logit probabilities, as in the Python code). The first step after a reset ignores peers; on the following steps only the bicycle utility is updated with the peer bike share of the previous step.

The calibration objective compares simulated and census mode counts: 0.2 x the DA-level weighted mean squared error of mode shares + 0.8 x the squared error of the island-wide shares.

## HOW TO USE IT

1. Once: run `prep_workplaces.ps1` (PowerShell) to create `derived/workplaces_xy.txt`.
2. `setup-data` loads the GIS layers, census and the four networks (slow, done once per session).
3. `setup-population` builds the agents, their travel times and the social network (re-run it for a new population; network paths are cached between populations).
4. `load-betas` reads `betas-file` (default: the coefficients calibrated in Python), or `calibrate` searches them (`n-trials`, `n-startup`).
5. `run-simulation` runs `n-steps` steps; `go` runs one step; `evaluate-betas` scores the current coefficients and writes `output/DA_calibration_results.csv`.
6. `relocation` moves `relocation-fraction` of the agents to a random new home and workplace and compares mode choices before and after (outputs in `output/`). Run `setup-population` again afterwards.

Memory: the four networks hold ~1.1 million edges (stored in arrays, not as NetLogo links). NetLogo's default heap (50 % of RAM) is plenty on a 16 GB+ machine.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

Bike use is rare and spatially clustered; compare the island-wide shares in the output area with the census shares.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Original model: VIRAGE project, ActiveTransportation repository (Mesa). NetLogo port: see PORTING_NOTES.md.
]]></info>
  <experiments>
    <experiment name="smoke" repetitions="1" sequentialRunOrder="true" runMetricsEveryStep="false" timeLimit="1">
      <setup>setup</setup>
      <go>load-betas
evaluate-betas</go>
      <metrics>
        <metric>count persons</metric>
        <metric>agents-without-peers</metric>
        <metric>item 0 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 1 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 2 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 3 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>last-error</metric>
      </metrics>
      <constants>
        <enumeratedValueSet variable="max-das">
          <value value="40"></value>
        </enumeratedValueSet>
      </constants>
    </experiment>
    <experiment name="full-baseline" repetitions="1" sequentialRunOrder="true" runMetricsEveryStep="false" timeLimit="1">
      <setup>setup</setup>
      <go>load-betas
evaluate-betas</go>
      <metrics>
        <metric>count persons</metric>
        <metric>agents-without-peers</metric>
        <metric>item 0 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 1 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 2 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 3 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>last-error</metric>
      </metrics>
      <constants>
        <enumeratedValueSet variable="max-das">
          <value value="0"></value>
        </enumeratedValueSet>
      </constants>
    </experiment>
    <experiment name="calibrate-and-relocate" repetitions="1" sequentialRunOrder="true" runMetricsEveryStep="false" timeLimit="1">
      <setup>setup</setup>
      <go>calibrate
load-betas
run-relocation-experiment</go>
      <metrics>
        <metric>count persons</metric>
        <metric>count persons with [ relocated? ]</metric>
        <metric>item 0 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 1 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 2 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>item 3 mode-counts</metric>
        <metric>last-error</metric>
      </metrics>
      <constants>
        <enumeratedValueSet variable="max-das">
          <value value="0"></value>
        </enumeratedValueSet>
        <enumeratedValueSet variable="n-trials">
          <value value="30"></value>
        </enumeratedValueSet>
      </constants>
    </experiment>
  </experiments>
